From e29a91e7b01bda0695599b18b3a39628965d31b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: barracuda156 <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 08:49:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 48/52] ppc32: generic wasm-to-JS wrapper conventions for a
 big-endian 32-bit target

G5 parade #8 of the 12.4 port, and the first RUNTIME item: mksnapshot
linked and ran, then died while compiling a CSA builtin:

  # Fatal error in , line 0
  # Unsupported operator: TruncateInt64ToInt32
  ... InstructionSelectorT<TurbofanAdapter>::VisitNode
  ... BuildWithCodeStubAssemblerCS / SetupBuiltinsInternal

(The text is patch 0036's VISIT_UNSUPPORTED_OP diagnostic: a Word64
node reached the 32-bit instruction selector.)

The builtin is WasmToJsWrapperCSA, the Torque half of the generic
wasm-to-JS wrapper (new since 9.4, src/builtins/wasm-to-js.tq).  It
reads its parameters back from the register/stack slots that the ASM
half (Generate_WasmToJsWrapperAsm) pushed, steered by three constexpr
bools from wasm-linkage.h.  Two of them encode "big-endian implies
64-bit", because upstream's only big-endian targets (ppc64, s390x) are:

  kIsBigEndian (= V8_TARGET_BIG_ENDIAN): an i32 parameter is read as
    TruncateInt64ToInt32(*RefCast<int64>(slot)) and an f32 return is
    written `<< 32` into its slot - a 32-bit value sits in the
    upper-address half of an 8-byte slot there.  On ppc32 the slots are
    kSystemPointerSize = 4 bytes and the value fills them, so the
    little-endian slot code is the correct one.  The Int64 load and the
    truncation are what landed in the builtin's graph.

  kIsFpAlwaysDouble (= V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC64): an f32 parameter that
    arrived in an FP register is read as float64 and rounded, an f32
    return goes out as float64, because the PowerPC FPU holds every
    value as a double and the ASM half spills the FPRs with stfd
    (MultiPushDoubles).  That is FPU behaviour, not pointer size, and
    ppc32 shares it (same kFpParamRegisters, same spill); with the
    constant false the wrapper would read the top half of the spilled
    double as the float.

Fix both at the definition site: kIsFpAlwaysDouble for PPC || PPC64,
kIsBigEndian for V8_TARGET_BIG_ENDIAN && V8_TARGET_ARCH_64_BIT (which
v8config.h derives as 32_BIT for V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC).  Torque wraps the
untaken `if constexpr` blocks in `if (block.is_used())`
(csa-generator.cc), so the Word64 nodes are no longer emitted.  No C++
consumes either constant (grep: the definitions plus the generated
wasm-to-js-tq-csa.cc only), so the object code of every other TU that
includes wasm-linkage.h is unchanged.

Rest of the wrapper contract checked for ppc32: StackAlignmentInBytes()
is one slot off arm64, so numFixedSlots = 3 = the stub's pushes and
kSignatureOffset scales; 6 GP slots of kSystemPointerSize then 8
8-byte FPR slots with no padding (the allocator's 32-bit padding term is
0 for 24 GP bytes); i64 params/returns take two GP slots low-word-first,
exactly as Int64Lowering numbers them (ia32's eax/edx pairing); stack
f32 params are stfs'd at the slot start by kPPC_Push on both widths;
returns go to r3/r4 (intptr) and d1/d2 (float64) through the builtin's
own descriptor.  The generic JS-to-wasm wrapper is not used on ppc
(CanUseGenericJsToWasmWrapper is x64/arm64/ia32/arm only,
Generate_JSToWasmWrapperAsm traps); js-to-wasm.tq has no kIsBigEndian
use and compiles under Is64() guards alone.

NEW CLASS for this port: "big-endian implies 64-bit" - code under
V8_TARGET_BIG_ENDIAN may assume 8-byte slots or 64-bit instructions.
Sweep (scratchpad besites.py, endian-gated sites in the compiled tree,
diffed against the 9.4 sources where mksnapshot and d8 ran on the G5):
154 sites, 142 present at 9.4; of the new ones this is the ONLY one in
CSA/Torque land, i.e. in what mksnapshot compiles.  The rest are
runtime paths: bigint.cc (de)serialization and turboshaft opmasks.h
generalize by digit type / byte offset; the ppc macro-assembler's *LE
memory helpers DO carry the assumption (LoadU64LE/StoreU64LE via
ldbrx/stdbrx, ISA 2.06; LoadF32LE's sp+4 after a 4-byte push) but are
reached only from Liftoff (off on ppc32, patch 0030) and the SIMD
S128Load64Lane codegen (SIMD rejected) - wasm memory endianness on ppc32
goes through the graph-level BuildChangeEndiannessLoad/Store.  Noted as
a d8-smoke watch item, not changed here.
---
 v8/src/wasm/wasm-linkage.h | 13 ++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/v8/src/wasm/wasm-linkage.h b/v8/src/wasm/wasm-linkage.h
index 5570874..1800cab 100644
--- a/v8/src/wasm/wasm-linkage.h
+++ b/v8/src/wasm/wasm-linkage.h
@@ -130,14 +130,21 @@ constexpr DoubleRegister kFpReturnRegisters[] = {};
 
 #endif
 
-#if V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC64
+#if V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC64 || V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC
 // Platforms where a Floating Point value is represented in Double Precision
-// format in a FP register.
+// format in a FP register.  A property of the PowerPC FPU, not of the pointer
+// size: the ASM half of the generic wasm-to-JS wrapper spills the FP parameter
+// registers with stfd on ppc32 too.
 constexpr bool kIsFpAlwaysDouble = true;
 #else
 constexpr bool kIsFpAlwaysDouble = false;
 #endif
-#if V8_TARGET_BIG_ENDIAN
+// The generic wrappers take kIsBigEndian to mean that a 32-bit value occupies
+// the upper-address half of its pointer-sized slot, which presumes 8-byte
+// slots: they read the slot as an int64 and truncate.  On a big-endian 32-bit
+// target (ppc32) the slot is 4 bytes and the value fills it, so the
+// little-endian slot handling is the correct one.
+#if V8_TARGET_BIG_ENDIAN && V8_TARGET_ARCH_64_BIT
 constexpr bool kIsBigEndian = true;
 #else
 constexpr bool kIsBigEndian = false;
